                "Dmitri had come, and Dmitri had gone, and Dmitri had left behind the evidence of his presence, the evidence that would convict him of a crime he had not committed.",
                "And Smerdyakov, when Dmitri had gone, had entered the house, and had done what he had planned to do, and had left, and had returned to his bed, and had pretended to be asleep.",
                "The town woke to the news of the murder, and the town was shocked, and the town was fascinated, and the town began to talk, and the talk became a trial before the trial.",
                "Everyone had a theory, and everyone had an opinion, and everyone was certain, and no one was right, for no one knew the truth that lay beneath the surface of the crime.",
                "The police came, and the police investigated, and the police arrested Dmitri, for the evidence was against him, and the evidence was all that they could see.",
                "The evidence was the money, the money that Dmitri had taken from his father, the money that he had spent on that very night, the money that proved, they said, that he was the murderer.",
                "The evidence was the threat, the threat that Dmitri had made in public, the threat that had been heard by many witnesses, the threat that now returned to destroy him.",
                "The evidence was the letter, the letter that Dmitri had written in a moment of drunken rage, the letter in which he had said that he would kill his father, the letter that Katerina had kept.",
                "And so Dmitri was arrested, and Dmitri was imprisoned, and Dmitri was condemned, not by a court but by the town, and the town was wrong, and the town would never know how wrong it was."
            )),
            EnChapter(3, "The Grand Inquisitor", listOf(
                "Ivan Karamazov was a philosopher, and the philosophy that he had created was a philosophy of doubt, a philosophy of rebellion, a philosophy of despair.",
                "He had written a poem, a poem that he called The Grand Inquisitor, and in that poem, he had told a story that was also a question, a question that no one could answer.",
                "The story was this: Christ returned to earth, in the sixteenth century, in the city of Seville, during the Spanish Inquisition.",
                "He walked through the streets, and he healed the sick, and he raised the dead, and the people recognized him, and they loved him.",
                "But the Grand Inquisitor, the old cardinal who ruled the city, saw him, and he had him arrested, and he came to visit him in his cell at midnight.",
                "And the Grand Inquisitor said to Christ, you have come back, but you should not have, for we have taken your place, we have taken your authority, we have taken your church.",
                "And the Grand Inquisitor said, you gave humanity freedom, and freedom is a burden that they cannot bear, and so we have taken that burden from them, we have given them miracles and mystery and authority, and they are happy.",
                "And the Grand Inquisitor said, we have corrected your work, and we have done it in your name, and we do not need you anymore.",
                "And Christ said nothing, and Christ listened, and at the end of the speech, Christ stood up and kissed the Grand Inquisitor on his old, bloodless lips.",
                "And the Grand Inquisitor opened the door of the cell and said, go, and do not come back, do not come back at all.",
                "And Christ went out into the dark streets of the city, and the story ended with a kiss, and the kiss was the only answer that Christ gave.",
                "And Ivan, who had written this story, had written it as an accusation, an accusation against God, an accusation against the church, an accusation against the whole world.",
                "And Alyosha, who heard the story, was troubled by it, for he understood that his brother was not merely a doubter but a rebel, a rebel who had declared war on God himself.",
                "And Ivan, in telling the story, had also told the truth about himself, the truth that he could not accept, the truth that he could not live with.",
                "And the truth was that Ivan did not believe in God, but he could not believe in nothing, and so he was caught between two abysses, and he could not find his way out.",
                "And the truth was that Ivan was a man of great intelligence and great suffering, a man who had seen the suffering of children and who could not forgive God for it.",
                "And the truth was that Ivan was a man who had wished for his father's death, and who had said, in the presence of Smerdyakov, that if there were no God, everything was permitted.",
                "And the truth was that Ivan was guilty, not of the murder itself, but of the murder in his heart, and of the murder in his words, and of the murder in his philosophy.",
                "And so the third chapter of the Karamazov story ended, with the Grand Inquisitor, and with the kiss, and with the question that no one could answer.",
                "The poem of the Grand Inquisitor was not merely a poem, it was a confession, a confession that Ivan made not to a priest but to his brother, and not in a church but in a tavern.",
