            EnChapter(1, "The Young Emma", listOf(
                "Emma Rouault was a young woman who had been raised in a convent, where she had been taught music, and dancing, and the art of seeming to be what she was not.",
                "She had read novels, and the novels had taught her that life was supposed to be passionate, and dramatic, and full of the kind of love that she had never experienced.",
                "She believed that she had been born for something greater than the life she was living, and she waited, with a kind of desperate patience, for the moment when that life would begin.",
                "Her father was a farmer, a good man, but a simple man, and Emma could not bear the simple life of the farm, and she dreamed of escape.",
                "When Charles Bovary, a country doctor, came to set her father's broken leg, Emma saw in him the possibility of the life she had been waiting for.",
                "Charles was not a brilliant man, and he was not a handsome man, and he was not a man of passion, but he was a doctor, and a doctor was a step up from a farmer.",
                "And so, after her father's leg had healed, Emma agreed to marry Charles, and she did so not because she loved him, but because she believed that marriage would be the beginning of the life she had dreamed of.",
                "The wedding was held in the winter, and the guests danced, and the food was plentiful, and everyone was happy, or at least, everyone pretended to be happy.",
                "But Emma, in the middle of the celebration, felt a strange emptiness, a feeling that she could not name, a feeling that the life she had dreamed of was still somewhere else, still waiting to begin.",
                "And so she entered her marriage not with joy but with expectation, an expectation that would not be fulfilled, an expectation that would become the source of all her suffering.",
                "For Emma had married a man she did not love, for reasons that she did not understand, in the hope of a life that she would never find.",
                "And so the first chapter of Madame Bovary ended, with a young woman entering a marriage that she would come to regret, and with the beginning of a life that she would come to destroy.",
                "The convent in which Emma had been raised was a place of silence and prayer, a place in which the young girls were taught to be modest and obedient, a place in which Emma had learned to be neither.",
                "For Emma had learned, in the convent, not the lessons that the nuns had intended to teach, but the lessons that she had found in the books that she had hidden beneath her pillow.",
