            EnChapter(2, "The Disappointment", listOf(
                "The life that Emma had imagined, the life of passion and drama and love, did not materialize after her marriage, and the disappointment was bitter.",
                "Charles was a good husband, in the ordinary sense of the word, and he loved her, in his quiet and unromantic way, and he did everything he could to make her happy.",
                "But he was not the man of her dreams, and his love was not the love of her novels, and his life was not the life of her imagination.",
                "And so Emma grew restless, and she grew bored, and she grew resentful, and she began to look for something, anything, that would make her feel alive.",
                "She tried to change her husband, but Charles was not a man who could be changed, and she tried to change her house, and she tried to change her town, and nothing worked.",
                "The only thing that gave her any pleasure was the thought of escape, and the only thing that gave her any hope was the possibility of a different life.",
                "And then, one evening, she attended a ball at a great house, and she saw, for the first time, the world that she had dreamed of, a world of wealth, and elegance, and romance.",
                "She saw the ladies in their beautiful dresses, and she saw the gentlemen in their fine clothes, and she saw the way that they looked at each other, and she knew that she belonged in this world.",
                "But the ball ended, and she returned to her ordinary life, and the contrast between the two worlds was unbearable.",
                "And from that night on, Emma was a different woman, for she had seen a vision of the life she wanted, and she would spend the rest of her life trying to find it.",
                "And she would not find it, and the not finding would destroy her, and the destruction would destroy everyone who loved her.",
                "And so the second chapter of Madame Bovary ended, with a young woman seeing the life she wanted, and with the beginning of the descent that would lead to her destruction.",
                "The ball at the great house was a turning point, the point at which Emma understood, for the first time, the distance between the life she had and the life she wanted.",
                "For at the ball, she had seen not merely wealth, but a way of being, a way of moving through the world with grace and ease, a way of speaking and of looking and of laughing that she had never known.",
                "And she had seen the women, the women in their beautiful dresses, and she had seen the way that the men looked at them, and she had understood that beauty, in that world, was a form of power.",
                "And she had understood that she was beautiful, and that her beauty could be, in that world, a form of power, and that the power was the power that she had always wanted.",
                "And so she had come home from the ball not as Emma Bovary, the wife of a country doctor, but as Emma Bovary, a woman who had seen the world and who had been seen by it.",
                "And she had begun, from that night, to live in the memory of the ball, and to live in the anticipation of the next ball, and to live in the dream of a life that was made of balls.",
                "And the dream had made the reality unbearable, and the unbearable reality had made the dream necessary, and the necessity of the dream had made her blind to the reality.",
                "For Emma did not see, in her husband, the goodness that was there, and she did not see, in her home, the comfort that was there, and she did not see, in her life, the beauty that was there.",
                "She saw only the absence, the absence of wealth, the absence of elegance, the absence of passion, and the absence was all that she could see.",
                "And so she began to look elsewhere, to look for the world that she had seen at the ball, to look for the men who would give her that world, and the looking was the beginning of the end.",
                "For the looking was not a search for love, but a search for a life, and the life that she was searching for was a life that did not exist, and the search was doomed to fail.",
                "And the failure, when it came, would not be the failure of a single affair or a single hope, but the failure of an entire existence, the failure of a life lived in the shadow of a dream.",
                "And Emma, in her restlessness, began to neglect her husband, and to neglect her home, and to neglect the duties that her position as a wife and a mother had imposed upon her.",
                "And Charles, who loved her, did not understand, and could not understand, for Charles was a simple man, and the heart of a woman like Emma was not a heart that a simple man could ever truly know.",
                "And so the marriage that had begun with such hope ended, in the heart of Emma, before it had truly begun, and the rest of her life would be a desperate attempt to find what she had lost.",
                "And what she had lost was not a thing, but a possibility, the possibility of a life that was worthy of her dreams, and the possibility, once lost, could never be recovered.",
                "And so the second chapter of Madame Bovary ended, and the chapter was a chapter of disappointment, the disappointment that comes when the world fails to be what we have imagined it to be.",
                "And the disappointment was the seed of everything that followed, of the affairs, and the debts, and the death, and the destruction of a family and a life and a soul."
